# EDMA v1 Unpublished Allocation Unwind

`tests/test-edma-allocation-unwind.py` creates a fresh output directory, reads
the exact DP pin from a local Git cache, and replays the baseline receipt's
001-012 patches followed by 013 with zero fuzz/offset. It never executes a
program from a prepared/archived tree. The supplied baseline C/header/register
files must match that fresh replay byte for byte.

The runner extracts complete allocation, cleanup and hardware configuration
functions from the replayed C. Other function definitions must remain byte
identical; only allocation acquisition/release and unpublished rollback may
change. Actual ring structs, register constants and SoC macros are retained.
Linux allocation, MMIO, reset and RX buffer fill are explicit host mocks.

The positive matrix covers every software-array/coherent allocation failure,
the stock DTS single-ring geometry, synthetic nonzero-start multi-ring geometry,
same-object failure/retry, repeated failures, every coherent position receiving
a valid zero DMA handle, and unchanged successful allocation/MMIO/RX-fill traces.
The fully allocated but unpublished helper is also called twice, with each
coherent position assigned DMA handle zero in turn. Actual rollback zero-handle
releases are counted separately from fixture success disposal.
An ownership ledger rejects unacquired/double/wrong-size/wrong-device frees,
nonretired descriptor/array pointers, leaked resources, post-failure MMIO,
streaming unmaps, packet frees and packet-store changes. Success disposal is a
fixture action, not execution or qualification of production live teardown.

Negative controls include the actual old source, decrement-only repair,
missing array release, uncleared pointers, skipped zero-DMA ownership,
unacquired coherent free and accidental live cleanup. Compilation failures are
fatal, never counted as detected runtime defects. Positive compile/run stderr
must be empty. Clang mode enables AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer,
`-fno-sanitize-recover=all`, and `UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1`; separate failing
canaries require specific sanitizer diagnostics and demonstrate halt behavior.
ASan's minimum redzone is 128 bytes so a negative ring index cannot accidentally
land inside a neighboring valid host allocation. The old TXDESC and TXCMPL
loops must each produce a heap-buffer-overflow diagnostic in sanitized mode.
Inherited sign comparisons, unused parameters/functions and unsigned-zero
comparisons are excluded from host warning errors; all other enabled warnings
remain errors, and successful compilation still requires empty stderr.
LeakSanitizer is disabled for macOS portability; allocation leaks are checked
explicitly by the host ledger, not claimed as LeakSanitizer coverage.

Example (run from the source checkout, substitute fresh output paths):

```sh
python3 tests/test-edma-allocation-unwind.py BASELINE/qca-nss-dp \
  --receipt BASELINE/receipt.json --upstream-cache LOCAL/qca-nss-dp \
  --cc /opt/homebrew/opt/gcc/bin/gcc-16 --output AUDIT/edma-gcc-fresh
python3 tests/test-edma-allocation-unwind.py BASELINE/qca-nss-dp \
  --receipt BASELINE/receipt.json --upstream-cache LOCAL/qca-nss-dp \
  --cc /usr/bin/clang --sanitize --output AUDIT/edma-clang-fresh
```

No make, target/kernel/module/firmware build, device access or network is used.
These tests do not resolve B-DP-002/003, validate runtime DMA stopping, emulate
interrupts, prove target ABI/build compatibility, or justify changing ring/IRQ
parameters. Generated C, binaries, logs, commands and input hashes live only in
the fresh audit output directory, outside production paths.
The allocation entry contract is a fresh or fully unwound instance with positive
valid ring counts, not reinitialization over still-owned live rings. Invalid DTS
geometry and concurrent allocation/teardown are outside this repair.
